      "dek": "The Japanese yen has strengthened again: it now takes 157.63 yen to buy a dollar, down 0.72% on the day from 158.77 at Thursday's close, a wider move than the 0.51% of two hours ago. This matters because for years investors have borrowed cheap yen to buy American shares and bonds. When the yen rises, that trade gets more expensive and some of it is unwound. At the same time American borrowing costs have not eased: the US government still pays 5.18% to borrow for ten years, on Thursday's official figures, which are the latest published. Today's yields appear after the US close.",
